window.DOCS_INDEX=[{"t":"21 NAND docs","u":"en/index.html","d":"We turn AI into the most basic logic gates and put them on a blockchain, so anyone can check them.","h":["Latest","Quickstart","Where to go","Further reading"],"x":" Quickstart Run the model machine in your browser Contract addresses Layouts of our chips on chain # Latest Model machine: writes in your browser 11-04 · A toy binary model runs in the page and matches the reference implementation byte for byte. The Bard: design files on chain 10-12 · The full layout is stored on X Layer; fetch it and check it byte for byte. 21 gates that drive 09-08 · Circuit #279 is written on BNB Chain, and the arena scores cars by the circuit on chain. # Quickstart Read the fingerprint recorded on chain for a file, then download the file and compute it yourself."},{"t":"Quickstart","u":"en/start/quickstart.html","d":"Read the fingerprint a file has on the blockchain, then download the file and compute it yourself. If the two match, it has not been changed. Read-only, no wallet.","h":["What you need","1. Install cast","2. Read the fingerprint recorded on chain","3. Download the file and compute it yourself","4. Try another file","Next"],"x":" # What you need Foundry's cast : a command-line tool for reading the chain. curl and a sha256 tool: built into macOS and Linux; on Windows use PowerShell. An internet connection: the commands talk to a public node and to this site's gateway. # 1. Install cast macOS Linux Windows Install Foundry (cast comes with it); reopen the terminal as prompted, then run foundryup Copy curl -L https://foundry.paradigm.xyz | bash Install Foundry (cast comes with it); reopen the terminal as prompted, then run foundryup Copy curl -L https://foundry.paradigm.xyz | bash On Windows, Foundry needs WSL; install"},{"t":"Check it yourself","u":"en/start/verify.html","d":"You don't have to trust us: recompute the website files, circuits and models on chain with your own commands. The first four you can do right now.","h":["What you need","1. A file matches the fingerprint recorded on chain","2. Run a circuit on chain","3. Check in your browser","4. Check the Bard's design files without a gateway","5. Check everything at once with tapemule (once the repository is public)","The Hub contracts are the pinned implementation","Every file of a site","Where you have to trust someone else","Checks whose scripts are not public yet"],"x":" # What you need Foundry's cast (see Quickstart to install it), curl , and a sha256 tool Everything here is read-only; no wallet needed # 1. A file matches the fingerprint recorded on chain What it checks: any file on this site. Against what: the sha256 recorded on chain for each file. Read the size and sha256 recorded on chain Copy cast call 0xd006ffdd5ae313b17729621a00999cd3c71ce5e6 \\ \"fileInfo(address,string)(uint32,string,bytes32,uint40,uint256)\" \\ 0x39d97ab281f04a5bc3aCc562f8DeA3f0b4266aA3 home.css \\ --rpc-url https://bsc-rpc.publicnode.com Download the same file and compute it yourself (on macOS use shasum -a 256) Copy curl -s https://282-732.aihashrate.stream/home.css | sha256sum When it passes: the two sha256 values are the same (drop the leading 0x from the on-chain one)."},{"t":"FAQ","u":"en/start/faq.html","d":"Is your computer good enough, do you need a wallet, can you buy the chip, does the token come with any rights.","h":["Do I need a wallet to check things?","Can my computer run the gate brain?","Do I have to wait this long every time?","Does a run leave anything of mine on chain?","Has the Bard chip been made? Can I buy one?","How is the Bard different from the gate brain?","Can I turn my own small model into a circuit?","Does the token come with rights to the circuits, income or chips?","What is the most useful way for anyone to take part?","Glossary"],"x":" # Do I need a wallet to check things? No. All the commands in Check it yourself are read-only. You only need a wallet to build your own circuit and put it on chain; see Build a small circuit . # Can my computer run the gate brain? When the page opens, it checks your computer first and tells you right away if it is not enough."},{"t":"The Bard chip","u":"en/bard/index.html","d":"A language model built directly as a chip. This version writes English sentences in the style of children's stories. They often don't hang together, the characters get mixed up, and a ⁇ (a word the model doesn't know) sometimes appears. We are working on upgrading it into a chip that can tell a coherent story; it is not there yet. The design files are stored on X Layer. It has not been sent to a factory yet.","h":["What it is","Numbers","Sign-off results (v8g)","Where it stands","Pages in this group"],"x":" Watch it write and fetch the design files in the app Fetch the design files from the chain (gateway page) # What it is The chip has no processor and no program. What the model learned is etched into the circuit. Turn on the power and it writes. The model comes from tiny-tiny-stories (MIT license). It writes English sentences in the style of children's stories; they often don't hang together and the characters get mixed up. The design uses only open-source tools. The process is SkyWater SKY130. The full design files are stored on X Layer. Anyone can fetch them and check them byte for byte."},{"t":"Fetch the design files from the chain","u":"en/bard/fetch.html","d":"The Bard's full sign-off layout is stored on X Layer. Fetch the two parts, join them back into the original file, and check the sha256.","h":["Where it is stored","Method one: fetch in your browser","Method two: download and check on the command line","Method three: skip the gateway and check by reading the chain","What else is in the same directory","Next"],"x":" # Where it is stored Item Value Chain X Layer Site 1.2.245.tape, path bard/v8g/ Container 0x75c6E2D063963561c08c838476111adda7a1a6CD File int_c16_machine.oas (full sign-off layout, OASIS format) Size 11,155,630 bytes sha256 3a03c0091f475c540fc6dcb022924ebfb03da13a10a62096d0c63cc4e404de0d Date put on chain 2026-10-12 A single file on chain has a size limit, so it is split into two parts: Part Size sha256 int_c16_machine.oas.part1 5,577,816 bytes 714c384d9d39e4639dd4ed53c2f744db2b3c4754614ec64a826a484c0b87c9fa int_c16_machine.oas.part2 5,577,814 bytes f2bc44baa5cb101a12985ee1f196f3e4077d9d29e2f1b38ccc74504d9584b7b7 # Method one: fetch in your browser Open the fetch page ."},{"t":"Process ladder","u":"en/bard/nodes.html","d":"The same Bard netlist, through place and route on seven process platforms. This page lists the area and frequency on each one.","h":["Two words first","Summary table","Limits of ICsprout 55 nm","Function check","Mask ROM area per bit","Tools used","Next"],"x":" The area, frequency and power on this page were computed by layout tools (OpenROAD-flow-scripts; LibreLane for SKY130) after a full flow run. They are not measured on a chip. The chip has not been made. Frequency is the result under a given timing constraint, not the limit. # Two words first body : the full chip logic, without the ROM that stores the model. 190,098 combinational gates plus 80,283 registers. Full chip : body plus 60 ROM banks. On processes you can tape out, the ROM uses mask ROM macros."},{"t":"FPGA port","u":"en/bard/fpga.html","d":"The Bard's full chip v8g routes on an XC7A100T with an open-source toolchain, with timing at 21.19 MHz. It is not on a real board yet.","h":["Results","Toolchain","Build","Timing recheck","Gate-level cycle comparison","Not verified yet","Next"],"x":" # Results Device Synthesis Place and route Timing XC7A100T-1FTG256C LUT about 45.0k / 63.4k, FF 21.4k (17%), RAMB18 107 + RAMB36 10 Routed, about 12 minutes, under 1 GB of memory 21.19 MHz, required above 16.7 MHz, passes LFE5U-85F (ECP5) LUT4 69.4k / 83.6k (83%) Routing did not converge, stopped on 10-30 — XC7A75T Estimated from the 100T result, LUT about 96% Not tried — The input is the FPGA RTL of the full chip v8g (the Tang Mega 138K version). Only the target device changed. Timing requirements: 50 MHz main clock, SLOW=3."},{"t":"Story box","u":"en/bard/storybox.html","d":"An ESP32-S3 writes sentences with the same integer rules as the Bard chip. Press the GO button and the text appears word by word on a small screen, and also comes out of the USB serial port.","h":["What has been checked","Model","Hardware","Build and run","Check on a computer","Run in the emulator","How to check once it is on a board","Next"],"x":" The chip has not been taped out, so the story box does not run the chip itself. It runs the chip's standard integer implementation (INT-C16). The standard implementation has been checked bit for bit against the chip's RTL replay and the browser demo. The story box has not run on a real board yet. # What has been checked Item Result Where it ran Firmware code against the standard results: C16, C32 × 3 prompts × greedy / sampling 12 token sequences, 12/12 the same; the tokenizer (SentencePiece unigram) is checked along with them Firmware source compiled on a computer, running host_test"},{"t":"Model machine","u":"en/machine/index.html","d":"A small computer that runs binary models. Each weight is just on or off, and this page can run a toy model.","h":["Run it here","Check it yourself","What it is","This toy model","Progress"],"x":" # Run it here Write 200 characters Write 200 characters Write 200 more Writing… Stop Use another .tbw1 File toy_bmlp.tbw1 · 102,400 B · 24-byte context · 2 binary layers of 512 · 524,288 one-bit weights File sha256 11c1c4b177a2308ee64732335335e044e4cf32ce0ddef75210b0d5fa4f5ed568 (registered value, checking…) Output sha256 0aef22dfb94c5a76f0aa1eba5ac3f3a1852ba1982a1965a5235b696579a7acca (200 B) (reference answer; checked after 200 characters) — Everything is computed in your browser: no server connection, nothing on chain, no wallet. # Check it yourself Look at \"File sha256\" above."},{"t":"Full-machine architecture","u":"en/machine/arch.html","d":"The model machine's main line runs in pure software on a small computer (SBC). This page covers FPGA plus ESP32-C3, a research line that has only run in simulation.","h":["Research line: one diagram","Parts","Three interfaces","Memory map","Multiply-add unit registers","SPI mailbox","Boot order","Two runtimes","The version without an FPGA"],"x":" The main line is pure software on an SBC : a runtime we wrote runs the model on a small ARM computer, and fetching firmware from the chain works the same way. See A large model on a small computer . The FPGA plus ESP32-C3 design below is a research line: we designed the circuits ourselves, and so far they have only run in simulation. No physical board exists yet."},{"t":"Fetch firmware from the chain","u":"en/machine/tapeboot.html","d":"At boot, the model machine fetches its runtime and model from a tape container. It checks the signature first, then each chunk's hash. If a check fails, it goes back to the last version that worked.","h":["Why you don't have to trust nodes","What is built into the device","Where it is on chain","1. Fetch","2. Check the signature","3. Check the hashes","4. A/B slots and rollback","Known limits","On a small computer"],"x":" # Why you don't have to trust nodes The holder signs a manifest. The manifest lists every file and the sha256 of every 24,000-byte chunk. The device accepts bytes only if the signature matches the built-in holder address, the sequence number does not go backwards, and every chunk's hash matches. A node or gateway that lies can at most make the download fail. It can't make the device accept wrong bytes or an unsigned version. # What is built into the device Item Use chain_id , registry , container Which chain and which container to read holder Holder address."},{"t":"A large model on a small computer","u":"en/machine/sbc.html","d":"No FPGA: run binary and ternary models with 1.7 billion parameters on the CPU of a small ARM computer. This page has the measured speeds and how we scale them to a board.","h":["Summary","Our own runtime, s6rt","Measured on x86 (llama.cpp)","How we scale to a board","Quad-core A55 estimate","JH7110 estimate","The s6rt runtime we wrote","Not done yet"],"x":" # Summary Model : Ternary-Bonsai-1.7B (ternary, PQ2_0, 436 MiB). Its quality is close to binary 4B, its size and speed are close to binary 1.7B, and it answers questions in Chinese clearly better. Board : a quad-core Cortex-A55 (with NEON) comes first, JH7110 second. Speed : the quad-core A55 scales to about 8–8.5 tok/s (our own ternary kernel), against about 4.9 tok/s for the original implementation. The memory bandwidth ceiling is about 11–13 tok/s. All board figures are scaled estimates. Nothing has run on real hardware yet. The models come from Prism ML's Bonsai series (Apache-2.0)."},{"t":"Gate brain","u":"en/gate-ai/index.html","d":"The small model BitCPM4-1B, turned entirely into NAND gates. Your computer's GPU computes the answer gate by gate, bit for bit identical to the model's integer reference implementation.","h":["What you need","How to use it","Some numbers","How you know it computes correctly","Follow-up questions: tested, and it often loses the thread","Progress","Next"],"x":" Open the gate brain Progress # What you need Chrome or Edge on a computer, latest version. It must support WebGPU (the browser uses the GPU to compute). 8 GB of memory or more, and a fairly recent GPU. Phones and tablets usually can't run it. The first time, you need to be online to download about 580 MB of model weights. If your device isn't enough, the page checks first and tells you. It doesn't download a single byte. # How to use it Open the page and click \"Get ready\". Wait while it downloads the model and builds the gate circuit on your computer."},{"t":"From graph to gates","u":"en/gate-ai/ncd2net.html","d":"ncd2net compiles a neural network diagram into a netlist made only of two-input NAND gates, and checks it bit for bit.","h":["What it does","Supported operators","How it is checked","Results (2026-09-17)","Next"],"x":" Open the netlist bench On the netlist bench, every netlist is evaluated gate by gate in your browser, and you can compare it with the floating-point model right there. It doesn't connect to a server. # What it does The input is a pyncd diagram with its sizes set (pyncd is an open-source Python library that describes neural network structure with string diagrams). The output is a NAND netlist in TapeOut format. Expand the diagram's structure into scalar integer operations (fixed point). Track the range of every value and give each wire exactly enough bits, so nothing overflows."},{"t":"A tiny language model","u":"en/gate-ai/tiny-llm.html","d":"impossible LLM v2: one netlist of 5,142,875 NAND gates. It reads the last 6 tokens and writes the next token in one evaluation.","h":["How to use it","How it works","Some numbers","How often it matches the teacher","How it was checked","What it can't do","Next"],"x":" Try it in your browser v1 archive # How to use it Open the page and wait for it to load the netlist (7.9 MB compressed, 36 MB unpacked) and the tokenizer (about 2 MB). Type a beginning in the input box and click \"Generate 48 tokens\". Tick \"Greedy\" to fix the random bits at 0. The same beginning then writes the same text every time. Everything is computed gate by gate in the browser. There is no backend."},{"t":"What a large model on chain would cost","u":"en/gate-ai/cost.html","d":"Running a large model directly in an on-chain processor on BNB Chain costs about 20,000 BNB per token. So the model runs off chain and disputes are settled on chain.","h":["Conclusion","Direct execution: per token","Run off chain, settle on chain: one settlement","What already works","How it was calculated"],"x":" # Conclusion Running a full large model directly on chain: we don't do it. Even in the best case, each token takes about 4×10^14 gas and fills the whole chain for nearly 30 days. Run off chain, settle on chain: this works. The model runs off chain, and only a commitment to the result goes on chain. If someone disagrees, the dispute is narrowed down to one instruction, and an on-chain contract recomputes that one instruction to decide. # Direct execution: per token The example is DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash (about 16 billion active parameters per token), running in the on-chain RISC-V processor."},{"t":"21 gates that drive","u":"en/control/index.html","d":"A driving circuit of 21 NAND gates, written on BNB Chain as circuit #279. Anyone can run it on chain themselves.","h":["What it is","Some numbers","The on-chain record","Run it on chain yourself","Limits","Next"],"x":" Watch it drive in the arena See the layout of #279 Contract addresses # What it is A small car runs on a closed curved track. Its front has 5 distance rays (left 60°, left 30°, straight ahead, right 30°, right 60°). Each ray gives 3 bits by distance (greater than 0.8, 1.6, 3.0), for 15 input bits in total. Output is 2 bits [left, right] : 10 turns left, 01 turns right, 00 and 11 go straight. The controller is first trained as a differentiable logic gate network of 13 gates, then compressed to 21 NAND gates."},{"t":"Arena rules","u":"en/control/arena.html","d":"Write your driving circuit on chain and enter it. The judge scores it from the on-chain netlist on 24 fixed starting points.","h":["Two tracks","Track and physics (v1)","Scoring","Inputs and outputs (v1)","How to enter","Decisions come from the on-chain netlist","Fees and prize pool"],"x":" The arena Full rules text (English and Chinese) The arena running now is v3: BNB Chain 0xeb248f4fb929686b045e63c47c69505cf0009888 , deployed 2026-09-09. # Two tracks Track track Circuit shape Environment v1 0 15-bit input, 2-bit output, 0 latches Original curved track, 24 starting points v2b 1 15-bit input, 4-bit output, 0–8 latches v2b environment, 24 starting points, at most 1,200 steps On both tracks, gates plus latches must total 1–256. The two tracks are ranked separately. Their scores cannot be compared directly. # Track and physics (v1) A closed curved track, half-width 1.3."},{"t":"Microduck","u":"en/control/duck.html","d":"39 NAND gates steer a simulated little duck robot. Circuit #281 is written on BNB Chain. Walking is still done by the original neural network.","h":["How to play","Who does what","Circuit #281","Results on the original field","Memory circuit #282","The new circuits (tested, not on chain yet)","Details of the check","Next"],"x":" Open Microduck See the layout of #281 # How to play Open the page and wait for it to load the robot, the MuJoCo physics simulation, and the walking policy. Pick a starting point and a \"brain\" (hand-written rules or circuit #281), then click run. Watch the duck walk toward the goal. The right panel shows the live input bits and which of the 39 gates are on. Everything runs in your browser. The page has an \"On chain\" switch, off by default . Turn it on and each steering step becomes a read-only call to the circuit on BNB Chain (through https://bsc-rpc.publicnode.com ). No wallet needed."},{"t":"NAND plays Squared","u":"en/control/squared.html","d":"A netlist of 386 NAND gates plays the 11×11 Squared mini game in your browser. The netlist picks its own moves to reach the target square.","h":["What this player is","How the page checks it","The on-chain button"],"x":" Open the demo How to play: click \"▶ Play\". The netlist controls the white square. Each step it picks up, down, left, right, or stay, trying to reach the green target square. Reaching it scores 1 point. Leaving the board, or not reaching it within 33 steps, loses 1 point. How it actually does: we played out all 14,520 positions on the 11×11 board (own square × target square) one by one. Every one reaches the target, every step gets closer, 7.3 steps on average. From the page's starting square (the center), all 120 targets are reached too, in 5.5 steps on average."},{"t":"Fruit fly connectome","u":"en/control/fly.html","d":"Behavior pathways picked from the connectome of a fruit fly's whole central nervous system, distilled into a few dozen NAND gates. Not yet checked by independent experiments, and not on chain.","h":["Data","How it works","The two pathways built so far","Not done yet"],"x":" Status: two pathways are built as gate circuits, each bit for bit identical to its model. None of them has passed an independent biological experiment yet. Nothing is written on chain. # Data We use the public connectome of the whole central nervous system of a male fruit fly (MaleCNS). All of it is downloaded: Item Count Neurons 176,422 Cell type groups 11,752 Connections between types 3,853,300 Synapses 125,024,863 # How it works Pick a behavior pathway with experimental support, such as \"take off and escape when something looms\"."},{"t":"Build a small circuit","u":"en/build/index.html","d":"Turn a small piece of logic into a NAND circuit and write it on chain, so anyone can run and check it. Five steps and one real cost.","h":["Before you start","Five steps","A real cost","Read next"],"x":" This is the general flow, not a step-by-step tutorial. For the interface and the latest rules, follow the TapeOut site . # Before you start A wallet, such as OKX Wallet or MetaMask, with a little OKB (X Layer) or BNB (BNB Chain). Start with a very small circuit: tens to a few thousand gates. A circuit has to be written in one transaction: on BNB Chain one transaction can use at most 16,777,216 gas (BEP-652; checked 2026-11-14), and at roughly 2,900 gas per NAND gate that caps a circuit at about 5,700 gates. What you pay for transistors and tape-out is a usage fee, not an investment."},{"t":"Worked examples","u":"en/build/notes.html","d":"Gate-level designs for TapeOut circuit problems: gate count, depth and a one-line idea for 10 problems.","h":["Rules","10 problems","The 9-gate full adder","Pitfalls"],"x":" # Rules Two-input NAND only. Sequential problems also allow LATCH (stores one bit of input each cycle). Gate count = number of NAND gates. Sequential problems also count LATCHes. Depth = the most levels of gates a signal passes through from input to output. Every problem was checked locally in simulation: small problems run every input, large problems run every input or many random inputs. # 10 problems No. Problem Gates / depth Approach 3 XOR 4 / 3 First compute n1 = NAND(A,B), then NAND it once with A and once with B, then combine. Exhaustive search proves 3 gates cannot do it."},{"t":"Proven lower bounds","u":"en/build/proofs.html","d":"The carry of a 3-bit addition needs at least 6 levels of NAND: 5 levels cannot do it, and a 6-level circuit exists.","h":["Result","Why 5 levels cannot do it","Checked by two independent programs","The depth-6 circuit"],"x":" # Result Item Value Function A and B are 3-bit unsigned numbers, c is a 1-bit carry input, output [A + B + c ≥ 8] Inputs 7 bits, 128 combinations in total Gates Two-input NAND, any number, can be shared, can connect to constant 0 / 1 Lower bound Depth 5 cannot do it Upper bound There is a depth-6 circuit with 22 gates This covers only this one carry function. It says nothing about the minimum depth of a full adder or multiplier. # Why 5 levels cannot do it List every function that can be built with depth ≤ d, and call the set F_d. F_0 is the 7 inputs."},{"t":"Agents-API circuits #283, #284","u":"en/build/agents-api.html","d":"The control rules of an agent session built as on-chain circuits: #283 is the first version, #284 adds circuit breakers, reason codes and tiered payment.","h":["What it is","The two circuits","What #284 adds","How it is proven correct","Sessions that were run","Try one call yourself"],"x":" Flip #283 and #284 in your browser # What it is The model, tools and sub-agents all run off chain. Each cycle, the circuit only decides the next step: call the model, which kind of tool to call, whether to start a sub-agent, whether to compress the context, whether to stop, whether to pay. The rules are fixed on chain. Anyone can use step() to recompute gate by gate and see whether a run followed the rules. Both are on the TapeOut CPU 0x6Fb4089e7Cbaa9660Fd11056274Cbd8117EE5B38 on BNB Chain, and both went on chain on the same day, 2026-09-11."},{"t":"Contract addresses","u":"en/reference/contracts.html","d":"The on-chain contracts and containers used for checking. All are public contracts; reading them needs nothing but read-only calls.","h":["Website file store (SiteRegistry)","Hub","Our processors and circuits","Containers"],"x":" # Website file store (SiteRegistry) Chain Address BNB Chain 0xd006ffdd5ae313b17729621a00999cd3c71ce5e6 Base, X Layer 0xd6efb7adcc9c83dc4924ad56f6a8e4e969b9adb6 Every file of a site and its sha256 are recorded here. Before reading, tapemule checks the implementation address and refuses to read if it doesn't match."},{"t":"TBW1 weight format","u":"en/reference/tbw1.html","d":"TBW1 is the model machine's weight file: a fixed binary layout. The whole file goes on chain as is, goes into memory as is, and is read directly by the binary multiply-accumulate unit.","h":["Overview","File header (64 bytes)","Tensor table entry (64 bytes)","kind","How BIN1 bits are laid out","scale_kind","What the multiply-accumulate unit computes","profile: tell the model by its tensor names","Example: the toy model"],"x":" # Overview Everything is little-endian. The file starts with a 64-byte header, followed by the tensor table, 64 bytes per entry. Each tensor's data starts at a multiple of 4096, and gaps are filled with 0. So memory bursts line up with 4 KB pages, and the file can be cut anywhere into the 24,000-byte chunks used on chain. Sizes and offsets are all u32, so a file is at most 4 GB."},{"t":"TapeBoot spec","u":"en/reference/tapeboot.html","d":"TapeBoot defines how a device fetches firmware from a tape container: on-chain paths, channel pointers, manifest fields, signatures and the check order. Draft v0.3.","h":["On-chain paths","Channel pointer","manifest","The `tapeboot` member","Each entry in `files[]`","`chunk_list` (large files)","Signature","Check order","Two ways to read","Slots and rollback","Client API","Known limits"],"x":" # On-chain paths Everything lives in the device holder's tape container, as ordinary SiteRegistry files. Path Content tapeboot/<board>/channel/<channel>.json Channel pointer tapeboot/<board>/<seq>/manifest.json Manifest, signed tapeboot/<board>/<seq>/<file> File; anything over 8,400,000 bytes is split into <file>.p0 , <file>.p1 ..."},{"t":"Commands you can check","u":"en/reference/commands.html","d":"For everything on chain and everything we publish: what it checks, against what, which command, and what you see when it passes. All read-only, no wallet.","h":["Set up first","Overview","1. Hub contracts","2. Every file of a site","6. Netlists of circuits on chain","Other read-only commands"],"x":" The repository of the tapemule command-line tool is not public yet, so the commands below that use it can only be run once it is. You can check a single file without it: see Quickstart . # Set up first Download tapemule and install its dependencies (needs Node.js 22 or newer) Copy git clone https://github.com/BruceLanLan/tapemule.git cd tapemule npm ci Run all the tapemule commands below inside this directory. Every chain read is pinned to a finalized block, and a result only counts when at least two independent nodes agree."},{"t":"Netlist format","u":"en/reference/netlist.html","d":"A TapeOut circuit's netlist is a list of two-input NAND gates: pairs says which two wires each gate connects to, taps says which wires the outputs come from.","h":["Fields","Wire numbers","Gates","Depth and cost","Outputs must be the last gates","On-chain netlist bytes (`nl_hex`)","Truth table bit order","Example","Invalid netlists","Not decided yet"],"x":" # Fields Field Type Meaning pairs [[a, b], …] NAND gates in order. pairs[i] = [a, b] means this gate's output = NAND(wire a, wire b). Required taps [int, …] Which wire output o comes from. Required nIn int Number of inputs nOut int Number of outputs, equal to len(taps) g int Number of gates, equal to len(pairs) d int Depth: the largest depth among all output wires C int Cost g · d³ srcId or id int Problem number."},{"t":"Progress","u":"en/updates/index.html","d":"What is done, what is in progress, and what we won't do. Every item has a date.","h":["Done","In progress","Not doing, and not promising"],"x":" # Done Date What we did See 2026-11-12 The home page now picks Chinese or English from your browser language: if you chose a language before, that choice is kept; otherwise a Chinese browser gets Chinese and any other gets English Home page 2026-11-04 Bard chip logic: the cycles needed for a long test (L3) dropped from 1,594,643 on 10-29 to 711,441 (the version merged on 11-04), and every word it writes is the same as before Process ladder 2026-11-04 The model machine writes in your browser: a toy binary model runs in the page and matches the reference implementation byte for byte Run it in"}];
